1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:09,840
Today I'm chatting with Alex Imas, who is Director of AGI Economics at Google DeepMind and Professor of Economics at the University of Chicago, and Phil Trammell,

2
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:19,920
who is Head of Economics at Epoch and research scholar at Stanford. In general what I want to understand in this interview is what economics tells us about

3
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:28,000
what we can expect in a world with more and more automation and more advanced AI. I want to understand what that tells us about what will happen to wages and the labor share,

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:40,720
what the best way to tax and redistribute the wealth generated by AGI will be, and what kinds of things will be scarce. What is scarce tells you where the value will accrue. I want to start there.

5
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:48,960
What are some plausible candidates of what will be scarce? Something like the relational sector, which is defined as

6
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:59,760
services and goods where the fact that a human was in the loop is part of the value of that product. Because humans are naturally scarce, if we have automation where a lot of other things stop being

7
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:11,200
scarce, we will still have scarcity in the things that humans are involved in and in the loop for. I'm curious to understand whether humans doing services for other humans can ever

8
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:21,600
be a big part of the economy. Here's maybe one intuition pump. In a world where AI can physically do anything humans can do, there's this whole machine

9
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:32,880
economy where they're building factories and doing research and coming up with new ideas. Humans may or may not be involved in the physical production of those things, but probably not in

10
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:40,720
the ultimate limit, if robotics is solved. If you don't care about humans being involved in that process, why would they be? But then there are these other things

11
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:50,240
you point out where we actually do want the ballerina or the barista to be a human. That's part of the value of going to a cafe or a performance.

12
00:01:50,240 --> 00:02:00,960
But only humans have that preference. So there's this human economy where humans are doing services for each other, and part of their wealth is flowing to other humans.

13
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:10,639
But part of their wealth is also flowing out, because they will want some of the automated goods this machine-only economy is creating. This is not a closed loop.

14
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:21,360
A lot of things in the machine-only economy are a closed loop because the machines don't care about getting the human barista to make them a coffee. Within that model, isn't it intrinsic that the

15
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:34,880
human-only economy will become a smaller and smaller share? I would like to pitch a rephrasing of that question. My view is that the individual forecasts economists like us would make, as individual

16
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:48,720
forecasts, are not necessarily very useful. There was a blog post by Andrey Fradkin, Brian Jabarian, and Andrew Koh that came out yesterday looking at economists'

17
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:56,720
forecasts about the labor market. What they found is that there's a ton of disagreement in every single direction. What they advocate for, and I'm in agreement

18
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:09,680
here, is that rather than thinking about individual forecasts, we should be generating prediction markets where you get aggregate forecasts and wisdom-of-the-crowd effects.

19
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:24,560
The reason I think this is because we have been famously terrible at forecasting. Let's go all the way back to 1820. This debate we've been having is actually 200 years old. David Ricardo is one of

20
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:36,080
the classical economists, not neoclassical. When the Industrial Revolution started happening, he wrote a bunch of stuff saying, "This is going to be great for everybody.

21
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:44,640
Prices are going to come down." But then he turned around and said, "Wait, I can see all these jobs that are creating value are going to be automated by these machines.

22
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:54,000
This is going to be really bad. Everybody's going to become unemployed, and there's going to be political unrest." And if you look at Ricardo's predictions,

23
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:04,400
they're actually right. All those jobs that made money in Ricardo's time got automated. If David Ricardo woke up and somebody told him all

24
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:18,720
those jobs did get automated, and then asked him, "What do you think the prime-age employment rate is in 2026?", I think he’d be surprised to be told it was the highest it's ever been other than 2000.

25
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:28,880
We have the highest number of employed people that could potentially be employed since 2000. That was the peak and now it’s the second peak basically.

26
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:41,360
What David Ricardo ended up missing is that you have these economics of structural change, where everything that got automated became cheap. People had more money to spend, and then they

27
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:56,320
started spending it on services. This is the lump-of-labor fallacy. David Ricardo didn't consider that new jobs would be created. But it's not obvious that money would go to services.

28
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:04,320
Why wouldn't it go to more automated goods and something like that? I'm not using this anecdote to say this is what's going to happen now and that we're

29
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:15,280
going to have full employment. I'm using it to say it's really hard to make predictions. What may be a really useful tool that economists

30
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:26,720
have is to instead start with a premise. Maybe we start today: labor share is zero. Labor share has gone down. What could possibly explain this? Let's write down an economic model of what happened.

31
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:38,400
Phil will talk about this later today. Or you can write down a model that asks, "What if labor share just stays the same? What can make that happen?"

32
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:48,720
If you don't take anything else out of this conversation from me: We don't have any data. I've been saying we need a Manhattan Project for data. We don't have data on consumer demand elasticities.

33
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:57,200
We don't know what they are. We're not really tracking what jobs are getting created or destroyed. The O*NET database, with all of the tasks

34
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:11,200
and different jobs, has been rarely updated and is super low quality. What is really useful is to think about the potential scenarios, map them out, and say what

35
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:22,080
dimension of scarcity will generate each scenario. If there's full employment, we can talk about the relational sector. If the labor share collapses,

36
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:28,240
we can talk about other sorts of scenarios. That will tell us what data we should be collecting. It's probably worth defining

37
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:41,760
labor share and capital share real quick. The whole economy, the total sum of goods and services sold, is either paid out to people in wages or it's paid out to capital,

38
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:57,120
which is to say, there's rent on buildings and shareholders of companies that get paid out. For many hundreds of years, ~60% of the economy basically gets paid out to humans in wages,

39
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:12,400
and the other 30-40% gets paid out to people who own machines and land and claims on companies. The question is, if 60% is going to wages right now, does that shrink as

40
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:26,240
AIs get smarter and better? This is a Kaldor fact. We should stress this. It's incredibly surprising that it's over 60% after the Industrial Revolution

41
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:40,800
and all of the automation we've ever seen. Some people are worried it's an accounting error that it's been so constant. There's even a controversy right now.

42
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:51,120
Some might say labor share has been falling in the last 20 to 30 years. But there have been a lot of accounting changes in the last 30 to 40 years.

43
00:07:51,120 --> 00:08:02,800
For example, Atkinson has a paper showing that if you keep the accounting constant over the years, labor share hasn't even fallen ever. But it's not that surprising, right?

44
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:09,520
Phil, you made this point that if labor and capital are complements, you need both to do anything. It would make sense that you'd need

45
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:22,960
to pay both of them to get something done. You have had stuff be completely automated. There's a sense in which nothing has yet been completely automated.

46
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:32,320
Look at the network-adjusted factor shares of a good. Look down the supply chain and not just the final step and how much of that is done by

47
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:40,400
capital and labor, but what went into the machines that can automate that final step. You'll find that labor is adding a lot of value down the supply chain.

48
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:50,400
Computer and electronic products in the US have a very stable network-adjusted capital share of around 50%. It's not 100%. I do think there's this qualitative

49
00:08:50,400 --> 00:09:02,400
shift that I think we agree is coming, which is that there will be at least some goods whose network-adjusted capital share goes to one. The whole supply chain can be automated,

50
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:13,280
and there's no part in it that we care intrinsically about having a human do. That will be a qualitative shift. Interestingly, the implications of that shift

51
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:26,000
for the overall capital share are ambiguous. Let's say we've got two sectors: the human-intrinsic sector with the ballerinas, and everything else.

52
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:36,800
Right now, everything else has been scarce because of the lack of labor in it. But if we fully automate the supply chains for everything else, and we satiate in everything else

53
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:48,240
really fast, then the quantity of everything that's not a ballerina goes to infinity, but the marginal utility in that stuff goes to zero faster than the quantity is rising.

54
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:58,640
I also want to move away from the ballerina example. The point I was trying to make in my post—working backwards from a particular

55
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:10,400
scenario—was that the ballerina and the performer are the wrong reference class. Right now we have a lot of jobs where you have different tasks.

56
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:19,280
This is the task-based model of jobs. Take a doctor, what is their job? They're filling out insurance documents. They're going and calling

57
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:33,360
different pharmaceutical companies. One of their tasks is to see the patient and talk to them, but that's not the main part of the job. You could have a job and a service or a good be a

58
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:43,760
product of different types of tasks, and you can automate a ton of those tasks. If the consumer is willing to pay more for a product or service where every

59
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:59,040
single task is automated except for that one part where the doctor is delivering the diagnosis and providing support, we would call that job part of the relational sector.

60
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:08,880
People are willing to pay more for the human to stay in the loop in the job. We don't have data to say, "Here are relational jobs, here are not."

61
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:21,360
You literally need to collect data of the following sort. Do a conjoint analysis of your willingness to pay for this service or good. Here's the counterfactual where everything is produced by machine.

62
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:32,400
Here's the counterfactual where this one task is not produced by a machine. What is your willingness to pay? What is your elasticity for the human to not be in the loop? If I don't have that data,

63
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:41,920
what prediction am I going to make in this story? Isn't there another point, which is that there are a lot of fully automated goods that don't even exist yet?

64
00:11:41,920 --> 00:11:49,280
And you can't collect any data right now about, say, how much people will want to keep buying more and more of some drug that makes you healthier that is fully produced by the AIs.

65
00:11:49,280 --> 00:12:03,440
Absolutely. That's kind of Phil's point. You could have an increase in variety in capital where you don't get the satiation. You're increasing variety, so you're not hitting

66
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:18,640
that diminishing marginal utility point where most of your income is going to the human sector. If that increasing variety is fast enough, and there is no such increasing variety in

67
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:28,960
the human sector, then you can get all of the relational goods you want, but it doesn't matter for labor share. It goes to zero. Phil, I liked your analogy to some Mongolian economist sitting around

68
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:41,040
in 1400 thinking about what will be scarce and the limits of that kind of analysis. I think you should talk about that. Just look at the goods available to

69
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:49,920
a Mongolian of the distant past. I'm no expert on this society, but I know that they didn't have nearly the variety that we have now.

70
00:12:49,920 --> 00:13:03,120
Look at the jobs that were intrinsically human, like being a singer. And then you look at the things that were not intrinsically human, like

71
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:15,520
the transportation services provided by their horses or the different kinds of food they had. If they just held the varieties fixed in both categories and asked, "What will happen once

72
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:28,000
we have a lot more automation?", they might have said, "We'll just satiate in horse-like transportation and in yogurt and in yurts. Those shares will all go to zero, and we'll be

73
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:39,840
left spending all of our money on singers." But of course, that's not what happened. As we've accumulated more wealth and more advanced machines, we've expanded the range of things other

74
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:52,320
than singers to spend our money on, and the share spent on singers has stayed negligible. Likewise, that's my central prediction about how the future unfolds, though it could go either way.

75
00:13:52,320 --> 00:14:01,240
I was going to make a point and I realize it's a fallacy, but the reason it's a fallacy is interesting. It's just hard to imagine a world where there

76
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:16,960
are trillions upon trillions of robots, but there's only some billion-odd humans, and the cumulative amount we're spending on robots is less than what we're spending to pay Magnus Carlsen or—

77
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:29,200
Financial advisors or doctors or tutors. Right, or podcasters or whatever. But then I realized why it's a fallacy. The number of transistors in the world has

78
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:46,640
literally trillion-X’d, maybe quadrillion-X’d. Your colleague Chad Jones has a very interesting result about how the share of the economy that is going towards paying for computing,

79
00:14:46,640 --> 00:15:01,680
paying for the transistors, has been decreasing. The point you made is that one way to think about Moore's law is… What sets price? Supply and demand. So not only are we producing

80
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:12,320
more transistors more cheaply, but also the value of the marginal transistor is decreasing. As you were saying, another way of saying Moore's law is…

81
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:24,080
I like the pessimistic framing of Moore's law: every 18 months, the value of computation halves. We're running out of uses for computation so fast that it's sustaining Moore's law.

82
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:39,920
This is relevant to a conversation about AI where maybe for the first time, this is no longer true. The famous fact here is that an H100 costs more to rent now than it did three years ago,

83
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:51,040
even though we have much superior technology and much more compute in the world. Because as models get smarter, the opportunity cost of compute gets higher.

84
00:15:51,040 --> 00:16:01,680
This is Phil's point about increasing variety. What we have done is increased the types of things that people demand from capital. Now all of a sudden you have a new

85
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:14,320
variety that you could be using capital for, and you jump back up. You could imagine we just never satiate demand for compute. As long as that stays the case, the share of the economy that is going

86
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:26,560
towards compute would keep increasing. That's the big question. That is the ultimate question that we need to be looking at. What number of new uses are we finding for that

87
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:39,120
compute where you have the demand for these uses? What I want to emphasize is that a lot of models in economics, especially in the space that we're talking about, take demand as almost exogenous.

88
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:49,200
They don't unpack the psychology of what people actually want. What got me thinking about the idea of the relational sector is work that I was

89
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:59,680
doing on the fact that there does seem to be this intrinsic value. It's not just because it's scarce; it's because there's some intrinsic preference

90
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:15,119
that people have for empathy, connection, and interacting with another person. One of the experiments that we ran involved an art print. We have an incentive-compatible way of asking, "How much are

91
00:17:15,119 --> 00:17:22,640
you willing to pay for this art print?" People are actually paying real money for it. Then we say, "Look, there's only one of those art prints,

92
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:34,640
and it's either made by AI or by a person." These are between-subject conditions. With one, you get the effect that the person-produced art print is valued much higher than the AI version.

93
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:45,120
Then, in a set of other conditions, we say there's 500 of these being produced. For the human-made one, the price goes down a lot because it's no longer seen as making

94
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:54,720
a connection with this one artist. With AI there's no difference. AI is already viewed as a commodity. We need to do a lot more research on this,

95
00:17:54,720 --> 00:18:05,440
but it seems that's the key difference between this and something like a horse. A horse was an input into an output, where you can replace the horse with something else.

96
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:16,320
You only care about the output. The only way this relational story works—and this is what we need more data on—is if a human is not a horse in the sense that they are providing

97
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:28,800
value from the output, where if you replace the human, the value of the output decreases. If that's not strong enough, and if it doesn't hold for enough sectors or enough jobs,

98
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:37,600
then this story doesn't work anymore. There aren't that many institutions that have thought as hard as Jane Street about how to turn smart people into some of the most

99
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:46,560
competent researchers and engineers in the world. This relies in part on an apprenticeship model, where new hires are paired with senior mentors. But Jane Street also runs a bunch of

100
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:53,280
classroom-style lectures and hands-on bootcamps. These courses cover a range of topics and they go pretty deep. There's one lecture that focuses

101
00:18:53,280 --> 00:19:03,600
on reverse engineering systems with tools like strace and gdb and another that teaches you how to profile code down to the cache hierarchy level. Importantly, Jane Street designs these courses not

102
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:12,000
just to teach the relevant object level skills. but also to impart the relevant tacit knowledge. For example, their week-long neural net bootcamp starts with general theory,

103
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,800
but then quickly progresses to how to apply neural networks to trading. And here they cover the specific obstacles that Jane Streeters tend to encounter and

104
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:23,680
the workarounds they've come up with to get around them. Jane Street takes this sort of learning incredibly seriously.

105
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:31,840
Every office has dedicated classroom space and courses are prioritized as part of regular work. If you'd like to work at a place like this, Jane Street is hiring.

106
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:43,200
You can check out their open roles at janestreet.com/dwarkesh. There's one possibility which Molly Kinder has written about, this "Messy Middle" scenario.

107
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:55,840
That possibility made me think about whether it might be better to have—at least as far as wealth distribution and redistribution go—a much faster AI takeoff.

108
00:19:55,840 --> 00:20:07,200
I want to ask you whether the following possibility is at all likely, or if there's any set of assumptions that can make it so. AI makes it possible to automate jobs such

109
00:20:07,200 --> 00:20:17,360
that many people are losing their jobs, but it doesn't create enough wealth, while the process of automation is happening, to basically pay off the people who are getting laid off and

110
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:27,440
create a Pareto improvement, where everybody's getting better as a result of AI automation. Of course, there's a trivial sense in which that must be true.

111
00:20:27,440 --> 00:20:35,760
Whatever money the company is saving by not paying the humans instead of just paying the AIs, those resources still exist in the economy and can just be paid out to people.

112
00:20:35,760 --> 00:20:44,640
But there's going to be some allocative inefficiency. The government doesn't know exactly who got laid off because of AI. There's a political problem. If the Meta worker gets laid off first and

113
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:58,880
they were making $200,000 a year, is there a politically sustainable situation where you give them a $200,000 check a year when there are many working people making much less?

114
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:09,120
Do you find this scenario plausible, where AI is automating a bunch of things, but there isn't as much wealth creation as there is automation? I think it's possible. To me, it does seem

115
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:17,200
like a pretty narrow window. My guess is that if we have the technology to automate so many jobs that it becomes a new kind of political problem,

116
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:28,320
then the pie will also be growing really fast. Well, unless in all of those professions it's automating, it's just a hair more productive. So the cost of all the capital to replace all

117
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:36,720
the software engineers is just a hair less than the cost of what we've been paying the software engineers. Why is it implausible that a company can save

118
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:43,600
money by laying off a bunch of software engineers? And in the long run, there's a Jevons paradox, and

119
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:50,560
we can't anticipate in advance what we'd do with more software, and surely there will be more uses. But in the short run, the effect is just that a lot of people are laid off,

120
00:21:50,560 --> 00:22:00,880
and they still need to figure out how they can use a million times more JavaScript tokens. Phil and I have been writing about these things, and we have mathematical models

121
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:11,120
in the back of these things. We don't have any political economy in any of our models. Andy Hall wrote a really nice blog post about the politics of AGI, and he made a really interesting observation.

122
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:20,800
If there's a 2% increase in unemployment, the political winds completely change. Unemployment has a huge effect on what happens politically.

123
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:38,320
Referring to Molly's excellent essay, I think in some ways one of the worst scenarios is a drip scenario because of the political economy piece. What you might see is people not really being

124
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:51,440
unemployed en masse, but moving into sectors that pay them less money. This is what happened with phone operators between 1920 and 1940.

125
00:22:51,440 --> 00:23:02,320
Phone operators were completely automated, but it took 20 years, even though the technology existed. There was this drip. It wasn't like this giant sector just disappeared.

126
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:14,560
There's a really nice QJE paper on this showing that they got reabsorbed into the economy, but at lower salaries, and they were mostly underemployed.

127
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:26,400
That's the scenario Molly was writing about, this messy middle where things aren't a disaster. We saw with COVID that the fiscal response can move quickly if there's an emergency.

128
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:35,200
An emergency is a quick uptick in unemployment, which could even look like 2-3%. That becomes a national emergency if it happens fast.

129
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:50,800
The concern is that whatever you're saving on those white-collar workers, if that's not growing the economy but just creating saved resources that can be allocated elsewhere, is that enough to do

130
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:59,280
a broad-based redistribution scheme? You have the money you've saved off a couple of people. Unless you can figure out exactly how to get it to

131
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:07,600
them specifically, you have the problem of, "Can I do a UBI off the money I saved by laying off…?" You're basically saying the pie did not grow that much.

132
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:19,680
You're just displacing a bunch of people, but that didn't grow the technological frontier of what the economy can produce. Then there's a question of whether

133
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:26,400
every time this has happened in history, the technological frontier has expanded a bunch. I think that's the case. Simply in history,

134
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:40,320
the technological frontier has expanded. I think Phil made the same point. It's hard to imagine that sort of scenario where you are getting intelligence that's

135
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:49,440
just enough to replace the software engineer but still costs a lot of money. It's just a hair less expensive than the software engineer, so you're not

136
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:59,120
getting this abundance effect. Where is the redistribution going to happen because the pie didn't grow? This is very helpful. Many different

137
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:09,280
things have to be true for this scenario to come to pass, each of which seem unlikely. One, it has to be the case that it is possible to automate entire white-collar jobs,

138
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:16,080
but only in a piecemeal way. That is to say that you can only automate software engineers, but that same program can't also automate

139
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:28,000
an accountant and an analyst and whatever. My model of intelligence is such that—both the breadth of tasks it requires to do something like software engineering

140
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:39,760
and what intelligence is—if you can really just lay off all the software engineers, you've got enough in the bucket there that you could automate all kinds of white-collar work.

141
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:48,960
There are huge amounts of potential savings that have happened as a result of these layoffs, and also AI is going to be cheaper than human labor. If both of those things are true, this messy

142
00:25:48,960 --> 00:25:57,120
middle scenario where we literally don't have the wealth to go around seems unlikely. Then the question is, what is the best way to tax it and redistribute it?

143
00:25:57,120 --> 00:26:09,280
I have some thoughts. I think it's really important to outline the costs and benefits. First, there's differential complexity in implementing these things.

144
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:21,920
Two, they differ in the timeline of being actually helpful. Something like universal basic capital is not going to generate returns for something that happens in six months. You probably are going

145
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:35,040
to end up with a layer of things. Take a negative income tax, for example. You implement it, and the day it turns into law, you already have this insurance that

146
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:47,760
there's a floor where everybody gets a certain amount of money, and if you earn more money, you get taxed more. But there are positives

147
00:26:47,760 --> 00:27:00,640
and negatives to a negative income tax. With UBI, for example, I worry a lot about the political economy implications. If people are just dependent on a check,

148
00:27:00,640 --> 00:27:10,480
it really matters who's in power. Right now, we're endowed with labor that can turn into income. When that is no longer the case and we

149
00:27:10,480 --> 00:27:22,480
are at the mercy of the elected official for basic needs, that feels like a power-sharing arrangement that's really dangerous. But wouldn't that be true of any

150
00:27:22,480 --> 00:27:32,160
sort of government redistribution program? With something like universal basic capital, where you have an ownership share and property rights for capital, you just have a share.

151
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:36,880
You're a normal shareholder. You're just a normal person. But this goes back to the question of indexing, because if indexing is hard,

152
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:45,840
then universal basic capital is hard. That's the problem of universal basic capital: targeting. What do you target to put into people's portfolios? Like, what if Anthropic goes to zero, but some

153
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:51,360
random robotics company takes all this over? Exactly. That's the risk of universal basic capital. With a negative income tax, you have the

154
00:27:51,360 --> 00:28:03,040
same sort of issues as with UBI, where somebody comes into power and says, "We're not going to do that anymore," and people can't work, and then you have the issue of the floor being gone.

155
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:13,680
One concern with the wealth tax is that there's no politically sustainable equilibrium at a 0.5% wealth tax. This happened with the income tax, of course.

156
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:28,080
It starts low, it’s for war or something, and then it slowly escalates until the marginal income tax rate in the US is on the order of 40%, and in certain states, upwards of 50%.

157
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:35,680
With a capital tax, is there a reason to worry that it would distort investment? Would people just say, "Why would I invest in Anthropic or Intel?

158
00:28:35,680 --> 00:28:49,520
The government is going to take larger and larger shares of it and dilute my share." Hold on. It's worth separating how the revenue is raised, what's taxed, and how it's distributed.

159
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:58,720
It could be that the government hands out shares of Anthropic to everyone by a broad-based tax and then buying Anthropic. Which would probably be the right thing to do.

160
00:28:58,720 --> 00:29:12,160
Hopefully, some populist proposal doesn't interfere with that and expropriate some particular company that everyone happens to know about. You're suggesting there could be some sort of optimal tax.

161
00:29:12,160 --> 00:29:21,760
We're taxing externalities or we're taxing land. I guess we probably need to tax something other than just those two things. Or consumption.

162
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:34,160
Ok, a consumption tax, like a European value-added tax, allows the government to go buy a bunch of stocks, and then they just distribute those stocks to everybody.

163
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:44,560
That's David Autor's... That's not going to be that different from just redistributing the stocks, but it will be a little different. That was the proposal for Social Security, by the way.

164
00:29:44,560 --> 00:29:59,440
That was privatizing Social Security. It's worked so far, but there are questions about how long it's going to keep working. Privatizing Social Security was basically giving

165
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:11,360
everybody a basket of stocks. People talk about whether there's a white-collar apocalypse already. Is there any evidence

166
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:24,640
that suggests there is mass automation or unemployment as a result of AI already? A lot of people are looking at it. This is an area where there's a lot of eyes and a lot of data being produced.

167
00:30:24,640 --> 00:30:35,680
The Budget Lab over at Yale is doing really good analysis on this. They just recently released a report, and you really have to squint to see anything happening.

168
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:47,200
If you want to take an approach across the entire economy, even looking at software engineering, the most exposed sectors, there's just not really anything going on.

169
00:30:47,200 --> 00:30:58,960
There might be a little bit of a signal about junior developers getting jobs less than before. But that's a "less than before" rather than a level shift, as in there's actually an increased

170
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:09,440
demand for senior software engineers, if anything. If you look at the trend, for junior developers, it's a bit below trend. So you're saying the growth

171
00:31:09,440 --> 00:31:19,200
is slower than before, but there is still growth even for entry-level software engineers. What do you think is going on with the anecdotal evidence of graduating college students saying

172
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:29,040
that they're finding it harder to find CS jobs? I think that's anecdotal evidence. You think it's always been hard to get jobs for some people, and now it's getting turned

173
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:34,400
into an AI narrative? Same with the layoffs, where it's probably just a normal layoff, and they turned it into an AI layoff.

174
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:46,320
You have to be careful with all of this. There are these public coordination devices. Let's say we get into a narrative where if you're a firm and you're not laying people off,

175
00:31:46,320 --> 00:31:56,960
then you're seen as not adapting AI enough. Then you're going to just get a cascade effect of firms needing to keep up with the Joneses in terms of starting to lay people off.

176
00:31:56,960 --> 00:32:05,200
That's super worrying, where the firm might actually be worse off after the layoffs than before, but it's just doing the layoffs to have the perception that,

177
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:19,200
"Look, we're not behind the times. We're using AI." You probably heard these anecdotal stories of token counters, where you have to maximize tokens and things like that.

178
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:27,680
Right now, we don't really have any evidence of a white-collar bloodbath. Is that surprising at all, given all these things AI can do?

179
00:32:27,680 --> 00:32:40,400
This is a story as old as time. If you automate some complementary task, the overall bucket of things—the human labor which complements the automation—will increase in value.

180
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:50,560
One of the statistics that's really important for that argument is elasticity of demand. Take the O-ring model of jobs. A job is a series of tasks.

181
00:32:50,560 --> 00:33:03,280
Let's say the AI automates nine out of ten tasks. One task is not automated. If that person can now focus in on that task, the job will become more productive.

182
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:14,160
If that translates into a price effect where the product is actually cheaper, and if demand responds enough where it's being bought more and the service is being used more,

183
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:24,160
that could actually lead to more hiring. A lot of people on the internet have been making that argument very generally, saying, "Look, if anything in the data, we're seeing

184
00:33:24,160 --> 00:33:34,560
an uptick in software engineering demand." Which suggests that at least for now, given the way that jobs work, it might be elastic enough. I think this elasticity of demand argument is

185
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:45,760
incredibly important for a lot of arguments that people make, or just a lot of labels that people use without understanding what the underlying causation is.

186
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:55,840
People often talk about Jevons paradox. This is the idea that as something gets cheaper, you will want so much more of it that the total amount you spend on the thing increases.

187
00:33:55,840 --> 00:34:06,720
Famously, this happened to coal in Britain ~200 years ago. But really this only happens if the demand for something is highly elastic.

188
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:14,080
There are many things for which there is not super elastic demand. If oil, for example, gets super cheap, it's not like magically— Or insulin.

189
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:20,719
Exactly. It's not like magically there's going to be so many more cars that now we're going to be using way more oil than before. At least not in the short run.

190
00:34:20,719 --> 00:34:31,600
Exactly. The long-run elasticity is higher than short-run elasticity. But even in the long run, agriculture famously is the example where we can produce way more food if

191
00:34:31,600 --> 00:34:38,639
we dedicated the same portion of the economy that we dedicated to agriculture in the past. We're already producing more food regardless, but we could produce even more if the same portion of

192
00:34:38,639 --> 00:34:49,440
the economy that was producing food 100 years ago was currently producing food. But you eat enough, and then you're done. The claim with software is that it is not

193
00:34:49,440 --> 00:34:58,640
some inherent property of markets that as it gets cheaper, you'll just keep wanting more of it. The thing about software is this is a particular kind of good where as it gets cheaper,

194
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:07,600
we'll want more and more of it. It is also highly relevant, and you wrote an essay about this—a lot of this podcast is me summarizing your essays back to you.

195
00:35:07,600 --> 00:35:18,800
There's this very viral scenario planning about the future by Citrini, predicting that as a result of automation and very powerful AI, there will be a recession.

196
00:35:18,800 --> 00:35:30,000
White-collar workers will get automated, their salaries will no longer be available, and so there will be a slump. Do you want to recapitulate

197
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:41,600
why this might be implausible? Part of it is plausible, part of it's not. The part that we started the conversation with is the idea that

198
00:35:41,600 --> 00:35:55,600
there could be a lot of unemployment. If the speed of automation is quick, people could get laid off, and they may not find work very quickly.

199
00:35:55,600 --> 00:36:01,840
We can quibble about the unemployment part of the Citrini essay, but that's not the issue. The issue is that they talked about negative economic growth.

200
00:36:01,840 --> 00:36:10,080
What I did in the piece, that Phil and I had a back and forth on, was to say, let's start with the proposition that there's negative economic growth.

201
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:21,440
What conditions do you need in the economy to get negative economic growth? It turns out the conditions are pretty improbable. One thing that you need is for the holders of

202
00:36:21,440 --> 00:36:28,240
capital, rich people basically… Basically what you have in those sorts of scenarios is a reallocation

203
00:36:28,240 --> 00:36:43,120
of wealth and income from lower-income people who are using their labor towards tech capital owners. So you need demand to be bounded, like a hard bound, not even a soft diminishing sensitivity.

204
00:36:43,120 --> 00:36:51,520
You need for them to eventually say, "I've had enough. I don't want to spend any more money." And for that money to not enter as investment.

205
00:36:51,520 --> 00:37:01,920
Then you can get negative growth. The crucial thing is, even if we don't want more shit, the world in which there's a singularity and we don't want to invest more money is crazy.

206
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:13,280
We're not saying, "Let's build more data centers. Let's build more fabs." Even though we have AGI, we're not investing in more data centers to run the AGI and that's driving more economic growth.

207
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:22,000
I sent the essay to Phil, and Phil wrote back being like, "This is pretty dumb," like my essay. He said, "You're trying to say that there's going to be negative economic growth,

208
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:31,440
but these are very implausible conditions." And I was like, "That's the point of the essay. These are very implausible economic conditions." That's where scenario planning really shines.

209
00:37:31,440 --> 00:37:47,920
You have the Citrini essay, which was great that it was written because it started a conversation. It's so intuitive, this idea that if there's demand collapse, we can get the economy to shrink.

210
00:37:47,920 --> 00:37:57,200
You could get that with a depression. In the Depression, the technological frontier didn't expand. Here, the technological frontier is expanding.

211
00:37:57,200 --> 00:38:07,600
You actually have abundance. For abundance to generate negative economic growth, that's really hard to get. Google recently announced Gemini Omni and its

212
00:38:07,600 --> 00:38:17,120
video editing capabilities are incredible. You can upload a video and then tell Omni to do things like change the background or adjust the lighting or add or remove elements.

213
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:24,240
All while keeping everything else consistent. But Omni isn't just a video editor. I got a chance to sit down with the research and product team behind Omni

214
00:38:24,240 --> 00:38:31,760
and I learned that it's a preview of how future Frontier models will be trained. It can take in any kind of input, whether that's text or audio or video.

215
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:40,320
And while it doesn't currently do so, architecturally it's capable of just seamlessly outputting images or text. So it's really a bet on the multimodal

216
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:48,080
data transfer hypothesis. The model becomes better at predicting one data type by seeing the others. For example, Omni is really good at accurately

217
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:55,840
rendering text on video, even though Google didn't specifically target that capability in this model. And Omni is the next step towards more accurate world models.

218
00:38:55,840 --> 00:39:03,360
Because in order to predict the next frame of a video, you have to have a deep understanding of physics and spatial dynamics. As Omni progresses, it'll be interesting to

219
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:11,600
see whether it can close a Sim2Real gap. Because it's much harder to collect data in the real world than it is in simulation, robotics progress has lagged other applications of AI.

220
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:20,080
But if you have really good video models that can simulate reality, maybe that stops being the case. In the meantime, if you want to try Omni, you can check it out in the Gemini app at

221
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:30,800
gemini.google or use it in Google's AI Creative Studio, Flow, at flow.google. We were talking a second ago about why there isn't more automation as a result of LLMs.

222
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:43,200
One plausible mechanism could be, as you were saying with the O-ring theory… O-ring theory refers to the fact that the Challenger shuttle blew up because one component malfunctioned,

223
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:52,080
and it destroyed the whole thing. Maybe that's a more general model of how goods are produced in the economy. You have to make sure everything is reliable and works well. So you can't automate

224
00:39:52,080 --> 00:40:05,120
an entire job to an AI right now. Even though it might be able to perform it at some probability, you need extreme reliability in order for it to not destroy the finished good.

225
00:40:05,120 --> 00:40:12,480
This might explain why there's a lot less automation now than there otherwise could be. But I think it works in the other direction once AIs get advanced enough.

226
00:40:12,480 --> 00:40:23,360
Integrating humans into the production flow of future goods will become difficult. Even beyond the arguments about how humans will be more expensive or less capable, there will be

227
00:40:23,360 --> 00:40:33,440
whole production flows organized for AI labor. They're talking in neuralese. They're thinking many thousands of times faster. So even if there's some comparative

228
00:40:33,440 --> 00:40:42,480
advantage where it makes sense to hire a human, there will be transaction costs and worries of reliability that will actually make it hard to integrate humans into future production flows.

229
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:52,000
That seems right to me. In particular, I just want to distinguish between the point that if you automate nine-tenths of a job, people

230
00:40:52,000 --> 00:41:03,840
might shift over to the last tenth, but there might be ten times more work demanded of them. Compare that to the model of O-ring automation from Gans and Goldfarb recently.

231
00:41:03,840 --> 00:41:11,760
If you can only automate nine-tenths of the job, but you do it to a lower standard of quality than the human could, you might not want to automate even those nine-tenths.

232
00:41:11,760 --> 00:41:25,840
That's the thing that could totally port over. Symmetrically, it could be a reason why we don't use a human for one-tenth of the job anymore, because a human just can't perform it to the

233
00:41:25,840 --> 00:41:34,880
level of quality that the AI can perform the other parts of the job, or the level of speed. They end up pulling down the quality or speed of the finished product.

234
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:43,504
By the way, the model you're talking about seems extremely plausible to me for why more lawyers, accountants, or even software engineers are not automated.

235
00:41:43,504 --> 00:41:50,720
There are cases where there's a pretty good probability that the thing worked as you expect, but the thing you're paying the lawyer for is: "No, really, my company's not

236
00:41:50,720 --> 00:42:05,200
going to go under because—" You're also paying for a lot of regulation-type stuff. With lawyers particularly, you need some entity to back up the product. You need ownership of the product.

237
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:17,760
You need somebody to be able to fire or hire, and there are licensing issues. There's a lot of regulatory layers that are also going to be keeping—even if there's

238
00:42:17,760 --> 00:42:30,000
no relational element—humans in the loop that have nothing to do with the ability of the human to actually perform the service. All of these frictions on the political-type

239
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:41,440
decisions that we are accustomed to only trusting humans for—legislation, being a judge, being a juror, or all the licensing that keeps certain professions

240
00:42:41,440 --> 00:42:53,440
human—that all strikes me as transitional. What we expect to come from a human and how we organize our politics has changed so many times throughout history, from little

241
00:42:53,440 --> 00:43:07,680
hunter-gatherer bands to empires to whatnot. Once an AI-run political system is much more efficient than the alternatives, those will probably tend to out-compete the others.

242
00:43:07,680 --> 00:43:18,160
Speaking of which, we've been talking about what preferences humans currently have and what impact that has on what kinds of goods will be scarce in the future.

243
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:27,040
But of course, we'll have different kinds of entities in the future: AIs. There was a time when there were no humans on Earth, but evolution selected for agents

244
00:43:27,040 --> 00:43:38,480
that have specific drives and preferences because those tend to survive the most, and those preferences now determine what a hundred-trillion-dollar world economy produces.

245
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:47,440
Why not expect the same thing from AIs in the future? This is not even a world with catastrophic misalignment, where they just kill everybody.

246
00:43:47,440 --> 00:43:57,280
But there will be evolution of, even if not individual AIs, firms which have AIs as part of them. What will that evolution favor?

247
00:43:57,280 --> 00:44:07,200
It will probably favor firms or agents that grow. There's a selection argument that things which grow will be more prevalent. Maybe just based on that, you can make some

248
00:44:07,200 --> 00:44:19,760
predictions about what their preferences will be. Is the kind of entity which prefers to have human-intrinsic goods going to be the kind of entity that accumulates resources the most?

249
00:44:19,760 --> 00:44:29,280
Probably not. Probably it saves more and has unsatisfiable demand for whatever the relevant resource happens to be. Compute is an obvious one.

250
00:44:29,280 --> 00:44:38,400
Can we use that to make some predictions about the non-human preferences that will be guiding the future? If there's an AI that has its own welfare,

251
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:52,000
is fully autonomous, and is making its own decisions that are welfare-relevant, to be honest, I have absolutely no prior that it would prefer to deal with humans.

252
00:44:52,000 --> 00:45:06,400
There's no reason. But let me take the other side of that argument. Humans' preferences to be interacting with one another, to trust and empathize with other humans

253
00:45:06,400 --> 00:45:19,440
versus a simulated AI, I think it's a really important question whether those will change. I've heard a lot of arguments saying, "Look, right now we're just not used to the technology.

254
00:45:19,440 --> 00:45:32,640
What you’re thinking of as relational… At some point, people are just going to see an AI therapist as a superior product, and they're not going to need the empathy that the human is providing."

255
00:45:32,640 --> 00:45:41,120
I think this is actually a really complicated question. Here's one argument for why it's not going to go away, and it has to do with evolution.

256
00:45:41,120 --> 00:45:49,120
Let's say there are two types of people. One person doesn't really have this preference. They can just interact with an AI, whatever can simulate it better.

257
00:45:49,120 --> 00:46:03,840
The other one has almost a moral emotion—using Jonathan Haidt's framework—against offloading those sorts of social interactions to an AI. Which of those two people are going to reproduce,

258
00:46:03,840 --> 00:46:10,560
find a mate, all of these sorts of things? I think the answer is clear. It's the second one that has the preference for other people.

259
00:46:10,560 --> 00:46:20,960
Depends on how the reproduction is happening. Fair. But if we're in the world where reproduction is still happening the way that it's happening, I think… And this is a big question,

260
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:32,880
I'm not making a prediction. You had David Reich on the show. His point on the last podcast was that we're buzzing with natural selection.

261
00:46:32,880 --> 00:46:42,160
So even if you get some sort of indifference now, you might get selection to point into an even stronger preference for other humans. Here's one way to think about it.

262
00:46:42,160 --> 00:46:50,800
How is the wealth of the richest people in the world instantiated? We were having a call earlier, and you made the point that their consumption

263
00:46:50,800 --> 00:47:02,640
is more geared towards relational goods. Like Mark Zuckerberg is hiring MMA instructors and dancers for his wife's birthday, and so forth. But most of his wealth is just stock in Meta.

264
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:17,600
As a controlling shareholder, he could say, "Meta, turn all this wealth into dividend income, and I will just spend that on consumption." Instead, he would rather have his wealth compound

265
00:47:17,600 --> 00:47:25,360
and have Meta build more data centers. So you don't even have to change humans for this to be the case. The humans who are wealthiest—and

266
00:47:25,360 --> 00:47:39,120
growing wealthier because their wealth is compounding—just have this almost Nick Landian preference for accelerating capital. That does seem to suggest that this is an

267
00:47:39,120 --> 00:47:46,560
important determinant of what kinds of things are produced in the future. There are two ways you could get the two kinds of people, one of whom prefers a human therapist

268
00:47:46,560 --> 00:48:00,880
and one of whom is fine interacting with the AI. If they both satiate equally quickly in capital but the one who likes the human therapist also just likes having some human-intrinsic services,

269
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:14,480
then the marginal value of capital in the future, compared to the marginal value of capital today, for each of them if they start out equally rich, should be basically the same.

270
00:48:14,480 --> 00:48:23,440
There could be interactions and whatnot, but basically, that should be the same. If what's driving the difference is that one person just doesn't satiate in capital because

271
00:48:23,440 --> 00:48:33,440
they're engaged by the prospect of exploring the universe and turning their head into a galaxy brain or whatever, and the other one satiates, then the person who doesn't

272
00:48:33,440 --> 00:48:45,520
satiate in capital is going to, if they're being rational, have a higher savings rate. So in the long run, they're going to have most of the wealth, and the overall capital share

273
00:48:45,520 --> 00:48:52,480
will basically be the capital share of that person's spending, which is going to be one. It's important that we're not talking about a hypothetical future.

274
00:48:52,480 --> 00:49:01,680
Elon Musk is talking about mass drivers on the moon. He's by far the wealthiest person in the world. Obviously, currently his investments are going

275
00:49:01,680 --> 00:49:14,080
towards humans as well as machines, but I don't think he cares particularly that his future researchers and engineers are humans versus AI. And he manages to reproduce fast as well.

276
00:49:14,080 --> 00:49:23,600
So I just think it's worth drawing that distinction. There are currently some rich people that don't seem to satiate quickly in capital, and so maybe

277
00:49:23,600 --> 00:49:35,600
in the long run they'll save the most. That does seem right to me. I would also say, even if they do reproduce more slowly biologically,

278
00:49:35,600 --> 00:49:49,440
that might just not matter that much in the long run if they can live forever. The living forever is key. Again, we're scenario-building here.

279
00:49:49,440 --> 00:50:02,240
If you could live forever, a lot of stuff changes for my story as well. To your point about rich people not consuming a lot and investing,

280
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:15,040
this will all depend on the returns to capital. Right now, the returns to data centers are super high, but if we get into a situation where people are satiated with capital, then the returns

281
00:50:15,040 --> 00:50:29,440
to accumulating capital are going to be lower. Then these rich people are going to be consuming more, because the incentive to invest is smaller. Basically, you think about the general equilibrium

282
00:50:29,440 --> 00:50:43,040
of this sort of process… We have gotten tremendously richer since 1820. Many more people are investing, but you're still getting a consumption response which keeps people

283
00:50:43,040 --> 00:50:52,480
employed and labor share high. That's because— Hold on. Wait, not necessarily. I think you're probably making the same point. It could be that their investment has to be

284
00:50:52,480 --> 00:51:02,000
titrated through actual laborers who have to do things for their investment to work. In the future, only the consumption is human-mediated, right?

285
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:10,720
Because the investment can just be done by the robots. So we're in the scenario of how you can keep high labor share.

286
00:51:10,720 --> 00:51:20,880
Let's take that scenario. In the scenario with high labor share, for whatever reason, the returns to capital are going to be lower. That's right. To the earlier thing where we were

287
00:51:20,880 --> 00:51:31,040
saying why the messy middle is implausible, I feel like we can do a similar thing here. For our returns to capital to be lower, the growth rate has to be lower, right?

288
00:51:31,040 --> 00:51:40,240
It certainly has to be lower than what we're expecting through the period of transformative AI. If there's explosive growth… Yes and no. The capital stock could

289
00:51:40,240 --> 00:51:50,160
grow quickly, but the price of capital goods relative to consumption goods could be falling faster than the capital stock is growing. It's the difference between the potential

290
00:51:50,160 --> 00:51:57,680
frontier of technology and the realized prices of these things. Because you have relative prices. So you're saying I could be putting

291
00:51:57,680 --> 00:52:10,000
my money towards earning 30% interest and investing in data centers, or whatever. There will be something in the future, if the growth rate is high, that earns high returns.

292
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:19,840
Or, as a result of all these technological breakthroughs, there's some cool product that I really want to buy right now, and both of those will be compelling options.

293
00:52:19,840 --> 00:52:28,800
Yeah. It doesn't have to be a new product. It could be a human-intrinsic product. Although, if it's a human-intrinsic product, we would want to have it much

294
00:52:28,800 --> 00:52:38,400
more in the future than we want it now, because the thing it compares against is— We might want it the same as we want it now in the sense that the marginal utility in a ballerina

295
00:52:38,400 --> 00:52:46,720
performance is exactly the same as now. But the marginal utility in a robot might just be a lot lower than now. So in units of robots, we want it

296
00:52:46,720 --> 00:53:00,320
a lot more than we want it now. Would the interest rate be 30%? It depends what you mean by the real interest rate. It might be that every robot now can turn into 100 robots next year.

297
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:10,000
So in units of robots, the interest rate's 10,000%. But if the price of robots is falling really fast... Prices adjust. I think that's the whole point.

298
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:22,480
Here prices are adjusting in this interesting way that too many macro models don't allow for. What's happening is what would be called investment-specific technical change.

299
00:53:22,480 --> 00:53:29,680
The price of capital is falling relative to the price of consumption, instead of doing the standard macro thing of saying there's just output, this chimera

300
00:53:29,680 --> 00:53:38,560
of a thing called output, which one for one can be allocated to capital or consumption. That's not going to be true in this world. Every unit of capital next year is giving

301
00:53:38,560 --> 00:53:50,160
up way less consumption than each unit of capital this year. One robot now turns into many robots next year, but the number of ballerinas is the same.

302
00:53:50,160 --> 00:53:58,560
Again, we're going to go back to the increasing varieties thing. If all of those extra robots next year are actually different varieties of

303
00:53:58,560 --> 00:54:13,680
robots and I'm not getting satiated on those robots, then it's a very different story. But now we're talking about the consumption world. For the investment side of things, there could

304
00:54:13,680 --> 00:54:28,000
be just some greedy titan of industry who keeps wanting more and more robots. That alone would be enough to increase the marginal value of robots and therefore decrease labor share? Yes.

305
00:54:28,000 --> 00:54:35,680
Why are we not expecting greedy titans of industry to keep existing? Greedy titans of industry historically have built libraries and—

306
00:54:35,680 --> 00:54:48,400
But that's because they die, and they're like— Oh, they all die. Everybody dies. Well, we'll see. Conditional on people dying… You

307
00:54:48,400 --> 00:55:04,320
had a guest on the show who said to understand the future, you should think about the past. You could have new types of titans being born whose entire reason for

308
00:55:04,320 --> 00:55:21,040
accumulating wealth is just to accumulate wealth. But a lot of the time, at least historically, the wealth accumulation process is part of a large social interaction amongst peers,

309
00:55:21,040 --> 00:55:31,760
amongst the community, where you want to be admired in some way. The stylized fact of titans of industry is you accumulate the capital,

310
00:55:31,760 --> 00:55:43,440
and then you buy a bunch of stuff. I guess this is a historical question, but it does seem to me that in a lot of cases what is happening is that as they near the end of their

311
00:55:43,440 --> 00:55:53,200
life, they either hand it off to their children, who are worse stewards of capital than they are. They don't even manage to grow their wealth at the rate the economy grows, much less faster than the

312
00:55:53,200 --> 00:56:02,240
economy grows, which their parents were doing. They're like, "Well, I care less about my children having it than me playing this game of accumulating wealth.

313
00:56:02,240 --> 00:56:12,320
So I'm just going to give it to some trust." If people are living longer or if they can figure out some way to align their trust to this wealth accumulation process… It just feels

314
00:56:12,320 --> 00:56:20,480
like the evolution here is so strong. You just need a couple of agents that think this way for this to be the dominant thing determining the preferences of the whole economy,

315
00:56:20,480 --> 00:56:29,360
because this part is growing much faster than the other parts of the economy. The part about satiation and diminishing marginal utility keeps coming up,

316
00:56:29,360 --> 00:56:39,680
I think it's really important. If a person has an intrinsic preference for accumulation, that's just what they want, then I think your story is totally right.

317
00:56:39,680 --> 00:56:54,320
But that's just not how preferences usually work. You have enough hedonics in your life, and then the social status… Rousseau wrote about this, St. Augustine wrote about this.

318
00:56:54,320 --> 00:57:06,160
This is a basic part of preferences. Now, you guys are arguing about something else, where you could have such high concentration that you could just have a couple of exceptions to the

319
00:57:06,160 --> 00:57:15,200
rule, and that's going to be enough. I have nothing to say about that. I think the claim is a little stronger, not just that you could have some exceptions, but that

320
00:57:15,200 --> 00:57:25,680
historically and today we see the exceptions. They just haven't really taken over the economy historically because there have been these dissipation shocks, as they're called.

321
00:57:25,680 --> 00:57:39,360
They've given it to their kids who squandered it, or they put it in foundations which spent it. It's not really a shock, but… People might have liked to

322
00:57:39,360 --> 00:57:47,520
fill the universe with monuments to themselves and live forever, very wealthy. It's a weird preference, but it's not a hypothetical preference.

323
00:57:47,520 --> 00:58:01,280
I think that's the claim. But who knows what's going on in their heads? Even without the intrinsic preference for accumulation, there are some

324
00:58:01,280 --> 00:58:16,560
instrumental reasons why some people might value accumulation, which is also worth bringing up. There's a desire for political, philosophical, or religious influence.

325
00:58:16,560 --> 00:58:28,880
People get into an arms race over what society looks like and what people believe. Similarly but differently, because it's not an arms race, there's just

326
00:58:28,880 --> 00:58:40,480
total utilitarian philanthropy. When I think about why it might be good to have a lot of wealth in the future as a good classical utilitarian, to me, the value—or

327
00:58:40,480 --> 00:58:52,160
at least one way you could have an almost unsatiating utility function in having wealth in the future—is to create new happy beings. They just add to the total welfare of the world.

328
00:58:52,160 --> 00:59:02,320
This idea goes at least as far back as Bostrom's astronomical waste point, that we could put Dyson spheres around the stars and turn all the energy into really happy simulations and whatnot.

329
00:59:02,320 --> 00:59:14,240
I think the particular greediness of this optimizer doesn't matter, what they're greedy for. Forgetting about utilitarian philosophy or whatever, a pure von Neumann probe has… I

330
00:59:14,240 --> 00:59:23,040
don't know, is this an accurate way to say it? They just have high marginal value for the random solar system they'll occupy because that turns into more solar systems.

331
00:59:23,040 --> 00:59:32,800
A von Neumann probe is a thing that can exist. That's a very greedy optimizer. If we're talking about whether they'll dominate the economy, maybe this is a technicality.

332
00:59:32,800 --> 00:59:44,960
But we only count final consumption goods and investment goods as GDP. If there's just this phenomenon— How does a von Neumann probe show up in GDP?

333
00:59:44,960 --> 00:59:55,280
Exactly. If we recognize it as a person that owns itself, and it's optimizing on the margin between spending a bit more on a baby von Neumann probe that colonizes another star

334
00:59:55,280 --> 01:00:05,040
system or a ballerina or something, and it just doesn't value the ballerina very much… When we're talking about AI beings, it just completely depends on

335
01:00:05,040 --> 01:00:14,160
how we're doing the accounting there. What does the world look like in a world where von Neumann probes are possible? Is it possible labor share is high?

336
01:00:14,160 --> 01:00:22,000
I think it's possible the labor share is high the way we usually count it. One of the biggest problems in RL right now is credit assignment because you have these

337
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:29,760
extremely long rollouts and you need to know why they succeeded or failed. One of Cursor's researchers, Sasha Rush, gave me a blackboard lecture on how they

338
01:00:29,760 --> 01:00:37,360
use targeted RL with textual feedback to deal with this problem and train Composer 2.5. I filmed on my iPhone, so apologies for the camera work.

339
01:00:37,360 --> 01:00:46,640
So we've generated this output. It's just a sequence of tokens. We're gonna send those sequence of tokens to this model that's gonna read it,

340
01:00:46,640 --> 01:00:56,080
then it's gonna isolate a specific turn that it says is problematic. Then we're just gonna do text manipulation. We're just gonna take that trajectory and we're

341
01:00:56,080 --> 01:01:05,280
literally just gonna smash in some extra tokens. After Cursor injects these hint tokens, they run another forward pass. The trajectory itself doesn't change,

342
01:01:05,280 --> 01:01:13,680
but the hint causes the model to assign lower probability to the error tokens. Cursor then trains the original model to match those probabilities, basically

343
01:01:13,680 --> 01:01:21,520
teaching it to downweight these specific mistakes. There's a lot more nuance that we couldn't include in this mid-roll. If you want to watch the full thing, I posted it on my Twitter.

344
01:01:21,520 --> 01:01:33,040
And if you want to try out Composer 2.5, head to cursor.com/dwarkesh. Do economists have any advice for countries which are not in the AI production chain?

345
01:01:33,040 --> 01:01:49,040
If you're not either producing the AI models, you're not producing the hardware that goes into AI models, if you're not Korea making HBM or Taiwan with the fabs or the Netherlands with ASML.

346
01:01:49,040 --> 01:01:58,080
India or Nigeria, what should they be doing right now? If you're talking to Modi right now, what do you say? I think the biggest lack of resources that we have allocated in the economics

347
01:01:58,080 --> 01:02:08,560
profession is thinking about middle-income developing countries in the age of AI. This is something I fault myself with as well. There's not enough people thinking

348
01:02:08,560 --> 01:02:22,720
about this question. There are scenarios where you get AI technology being allocated and dissipating to Nigeria and developing countries,

349
01:02:22,720 --> 01:02:32,320
leveling the playing field, essentially giving them a level up as far as capabilities. But there's another world where, because they don't have enough resources, they're not training

350
01:02:32,320 --> 01:02:43,920
the models, they don't have the hardware, and they just completely get left behind. And because of automation, we can produce commodities in developed countries now.

351
01:02:43,920 --> 01:02:54,880
Then we don't even have the consumer market. That world looks pretty bad. This seems to me like an extension of the messy middle case.

352
01:02:54,880 --> 01:03:06,000
One of the ways in which the messy middle might only be bad in a narrow range of scenarios isn't just that it would be easy to redistribute because the pie would be bigger, but because the interest

353
01:03:06,000 --> 01:03:17,600
rate would be way higher, and/or, equivalently, the price of everything except human-intrinsic goods would be falling really rapidly. They’re sort of two sides of the same coin.

354
01:03:17,600 --> 01:03:26,720
A little bit of savings would turn into a lot of consumption next year. Things have to go really wrong for us to just get over the threshold of

355
01:03:26,720 --> 01:03:36,400
capital being productive enough to automate lots of work, but not be productive enough that the interest rate is high and the price of capital-produced goods is falling a lot.

356
01:03:36,400 --> 01:03:43,600
Even without redistribution, a little bit of savings will save a lot of people. You're saying if the developing countries have some savings in the developed world,

357
01:03:43,600 --> 01:03:49,360
that will be enough to produce a lot of surplus that they can then— They will now be able to consume a lot using their savings.

358
01:03:49,360 --> 01:04:02,400
But the messy middle could be wider in this case. They're starting from such a lower level in terms of how much they have and how much it's actually indexed to the global economy.

359
01:04:02,400 --> 01:04:13,520
I think it's important for them to get on it now. I don't have strong feelings about whether it should take the form of sovereign wealth funds that invest in the right supply chains or

360
01:04:13,520 --> 01:04:19,600
just subsidies to their own citizens to buy a little bit of— This is actually a crucial point. We were talking earlier about why

361
01:04:19,600 --> 01:04:27,040
the Rockefellers of the world, why their descendants don't control everything, if our argument about the selection of these greedy optimizers holds.

362
01:04:27,040 --> 01:04:33,280
One argument is just that it's very hard to index the economy. Maybe they would've just decided to have their heirs index the economy and have

363
01:04:33,280 --> 01:04:48,560
their wealth grow at the rate of economic growth, and their heirs would be trillionaires by now. Before index funds existed, it was just very hard. A very small fraction of the economy,

364
01:04:48,560 --> 01:04:59,040
going back 100 years, accounts for a majority of the value created now. If you missed those particular things, your wealth would've just stagnated.

365
01:04:59,040 --> 01:05:07,360
Maybe there was a brief golden window from the creation of index funds up until five years ago where you could actually index the economy and have

366
01:05:07,360 --> 01:05:19,040
your wealth grow at the rate the economy grows. But now we're in this world with very concentrated returns, especially to private companies. As we were making the point in our blog post,

367
01:05:19,040 --> 01:05:32,080
this is capital that the average person has disproportionately less access to. Most of their capital is having a random house, at least in the US. Or a part of a house. Which, as we were saying,

368
01:05:32,080 --> 01:05:44,160
is capital that is uniquely ill-suited to be complementary to the production of AI or the serving of AI or to robots. Or the kinds of goods that

369
01:05:44,160 --> 01:05:56,000
the rich will bid up the prices of. Exactly. What is the value of a house currently? It's that the land is close to other humans and modulo relational stuff that is just not going to

370
01:05:56,000 --> 01:06:05,120
be the main factor of production in the future. This would be why a Georgist tax would not raise enough money for the sort of programs that we will be discussing.

371
01:06:05,120 --> 01:06:16,720
Right. But stepping back, the point I was trying to make is, if it gets harder to index the economy now, and that is the main way in which normal people are supposed to—modulo some sort

372
01:06:16,720 --> 01:06:28,240
of universal basic income— In the developed world. —are supposed to have some purchase on the wealth from AI. And it's also the way that developing countries are supposed to have some

373
01:06:28,240 --> 01:06:38,080
purchase on the wealth gains from AI. But it's very hard. Does Nigeria own a lot of SK Hynix and Anthropic? I'm guessing not. It's not

374
01:06:38,080 --> 01:06:53,280
enough for them to just own the S&P 500. This brings up a really important point. Is AI going to be like electricity or social media? Think about ConEd, or whatever the electricity provider here is.

375
01:06:53,280 --> 01:07:04,560
It's a monopoly. It provides a resource that everybody uses. But do we think about electricity as creating a concentration of power?

376
01:07:04,560 --> 01:07:14,960
Does ConEd have this huge amount of political power, social power, or something like that? No, because with electricity, a lot of the downstream benefits actually came

377
01:07:14,960 --> 01:07:23,920
to the users of the electricity rather than the actual entity producing it. On the other hand, with social media, it was the opposite case.

378
01:07:23,920 --> 01:07:35,200
Social media was everywhere. Everybody uses social media, but the rents went to the platform. That's a really interesting point. I don't endorse this take yet,

379
01:07:35,200 --> 01:07:46,960
I'm going to talk out loud. The more you think our economy is going to be run on AGI the way our economy currently runs on electricity—that is,

380
01:07:46,960 --> 01:07:56,400
there's a broad fundamental transformation of the entire economy—the more it looks like electricity… Every company in the S&P of the future, if it's going to make it to the S&P 500,

381
01:07:56,400 --> 01:08:08,800
it is because it has leveraged AI. Exactly. And then you're indexed again. But then again, I guess if you just look at how concentrated the S&P is over time,

382
01:08:08,800 --> 01:08:18,080
just these big tech companies much more so… I guess this goes to a fundamental point that it's hard to reason about how much of the gains from AI these individual private

383
01:08:18,080 --> 01:08:28,399
companies will be able to control. I think the open model thing is going to be a big point here. If we're indeed in a world where

384
01:08:28,399 --> 01:08:39,359
the open models are six months behind the frontier—or nine months—then we'll hit AGI, we'll hit whatever, and in six months, everybody has access to this resource.

385
01:08:39,359 --> 01:08:44,880
This goes to show you that every question is connected to every other. That question about whether there's runaway gains connects to questions

386
01:08:44,880 --> 01:08:52,000
about recursive self-improvement. Even if not recursive self-improvement, then continual learning, or online learning, which lets a model learn on the job.

387
01:08:52,000 --> 01:09:09,600
So if it's deployed, it gets to learn more. These are just forecasting technical questions which then impact whether Uganda will have any purchase on the returns of AGI.

388
01:09:09,600 --> 01:09:17,120
The reason I'm emphasizing the question is I think both for the messy middle and for developing countries, a recommendation that is often made naively is that you've

389
01:09:17,120 --> 01:09:25,040
got to do some kind of retraining. You've got to do some kind of jobs program, or you've got to have them build data centers in your country.

390
01:09:25,040 --> 01:09:35,439
I think you guys are suggesting something closer to just buying the index of AGI. That's probably a much cleaner strategy and much more likely to succeed.

391
01:09:35,439 --> 01:09:44,720
These are the two scenarios. I think there is a world where it is concentrated, in which case it's going to be really hard to index AGI.

392
01:09:44,720 --> 01:09:57,440
There is another world where it is electricity. Basically every company has access to AGI. So you just buy the index. Nigeria just needs to buy the index, and Nigeria

393
01:09:57,440 --> 01:10:12,560
has access to AGI because of the open models. Just to get back to the question about whether to go with retraining or trying to index. I would prioritize trying to index,

394
01:10:12,560 --> 01:10:30,400
just given how fast AI could hit the world. But I definitely wouldn't just rely on that. In the messy middle cases or the long-timeline cases where we don't get anything like AGI

395
01:10:30,400 --> 01:10:42,800
all that soon, it would be leaving a lot of value on the table if you could have retrained to be a bit better educated on how to use the latest wave of computing.

396
01:10:42,800 --> 01:10:50,720
I don't think there's that much of an either/or there. Maybe the reason to be pessimistic about this is because one of the reasons a country is poor is

397
01:10:50,720 --> 01:11:02,240
that it has a bad education system, so becoming the best in the world at retraining people at using AI doesn't seem like a particularly promising strategy for that poor country.

398
01:11:02,240 --> 01:11:14,000
Although there are cases where, in developing countries, you had this leapfrogging effect with, for example, mobile banking. It's much more prevalent in

399
01:11:14,000 --> 01:11:24,800
Nigeria than it is in Germany. Everybody is doing mobile banking. They have it on their phones, and they're constantly doing this sort of thing.

400
01:11:24,800 --> 01:11:38,800
Again, I'm not putting probabilities on this, but with a transformative technology like AI, you could get leapfrogging where you skip the step in the middle and get really astronomical growth.

401
01:11:38,800 --> 01:11:50,640
Just about the ease of indexing, I think it's definitely something to worry about a bit and keep an eye on. But as discussed in our own essay,

402
01:11:50,640 --> 01:11:58,960
and as other people have pointed out, it's already not that hard to index. There's been a bit of an increase in the privatization of returns, but still,

403
01:11:58,960 --> 01:12:14,480
well under 20% of the total market cap of non-tiny companies in the US is private. Everyone thinks about OpenAI and Anthropic. If that's where all the wealth will accrue,

404
01:12:14,480 --> 01:12:23,440
then all these questions about whether open models will stay only a little bit behind, those are important. But even they look like

405
01:12:23,440 --> 01:12:32,640
they're going public before too long, probably. The frictions that have been keeping companies from going public might themselves be alleviated by AI a lot, just all of the

406
01:12:32,640 --> 01:12:42,320
disclosure requirements and whatnot. They want to get access to more potential investors, too. If I had to guess,

407
01:12:42,320 --> 01:12:54,080
I would guess that the long general trend of lowering those frictions and making it easier for more and more people to index will continue, despite the recent bump in the other direction.

408
01:12:54,080 --> 01:13:06,240
This actually makes me hope even more so than before that the labs do get commoditized, or at the very least they go public as soon as possible. But hopefully they just get totally commoditized.

409
01:13:06,240 --> 01:13:20,800
I think AI will be much more popular and, more importantly, will be much more likely to lead to broad increases in prosperity if it is as hard to capture the gains of AI as it is to capture

410
01:13:20,800 --> 01:13:35,680
the gains of electrification. Exactly. There's no anti-electricity people out there. I mean electricity doesn't take your job, but— Well, it takes some people's jobs. It took some people's jobs, yeah.

411
01:13:35,680 --> 01:13:47,440
This is maybe tangential to the conversation but I think narratives matter. There's this really negative narrative around AI right now, but that's because people are

412
01:13:47,440 --> 01:14:00,400
not putting out the positive narrative. There’s a reason. It's more difficult to imagine a good thing that doesn't exist than losing something that exists.

413
01:14:00,400 --> 01:14:10,960
It's much easier for somebody to go on a podcast and say, "These jobs that you like, they're going away," than for somebody to spin up a utopia which doesn't exist yet.

414
01:14:10,960 --> 01:14:24,640
I hope this isn't too out of left field, but I would be remiss if I didn't point out one big cost of having commoditized frontier AI models, which is the tech race dynamic.

415
01:14:24,640 --> 01:14:33,120
For safety purposes, you might want fewer frontier companies so that each one has a buffer in case they want to slow things down to make things safer.

416
01:14:33,120 --> 01:14:45,360
The way this relates to our point before about the widespread access of the returns, is that I think there's a lot less of a trade-off there than some people imagine.

417
01:14:45,360 --> 01:14:58,160
Some people think either frontier AI gets commoditized and we all enjoy the benefits—but there might be some risk, because the market's really competitive and cutthroat—or

418
01:14:58,160 --> 01:15:06,000
things are safer because there's a big gap between the leader and the laggard. But that means the leaders get fantastically wealthy?

419
01:15:06,000 --> 01:15:15,600
No. You could just have a relatively big gap, but it's a public company, and ownership in it is widely distributed. More recently, I have been thinking that

420
01:15:15,600 --> 01:15:31,920
the risk of commodification—which is that it diffuses the ability to use AI to harmful ends—is worth the benefit. I worry that having these concentrated labs

421
01:15:31,920 --> 01:15:44,000
not only makes it so that the surplus isn't as widely distributed through society, but also creates a very tangible, clear political target for the government.

422
01:15:44,000 --> 01:15:54,400
We saw this with the Defense Production Act threat against Anthropic. If there wasn't one lab, or a couple of labs, that are clearly ahead of others, this kind of threat would be much harder to make.

423
01:15:54,400 --> 01:16:06,160
Thank you guys for doing this. I feel like there's a lot of unresolved questions, but it is helpful to know what the first branch is along all these important dimensions. Great. Thank you.
